WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.240
KERRY ST. PÉ: It's the wetlands that make our people what they are.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.600
It's really all about the people.

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:19.340
It's the wetlands that are responsible for forging the culture that we have.

4
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.340
We're all bayou people, we're all marsh people, we're all wetlands people.

5
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:32.860
We have this rich mixture of cultures intertwined and that's the thing that makes this place unique, and it's because of the wetlands.

6
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.720
That's why people come here and that's why people like to stay.

7
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.960
So it's all about the wetlands.

8
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.100
The wetlands are us and we are the wetlands and that's why it's so important to save it.

9
00:00:45.100 --> 00:01:05.200
There's a long list of things we could lose if we weren't restored, a long list of fisheries products: oysters, crabs, shrimp.

10
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.240
But most importantly we'd lose the culture.

11
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.380
There's no culture like Southeast Louisiana.

12
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:17.680
I mean, people like to come here for a reason.

13
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.660
It's different ecologically.

14
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:34.500
We live at the end of one of the world's great rivers so it would follow that we would be different ecologically but we're different culturally also.

15
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.900
And that's what I fear most, we lose this culture.

16
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.180
So, we need to restore it for the culture, for the people.
